1. e4 {[from the parent game] The king's pawn, two moves.} e5 {[from the parent game] The same.}
2. Bc4 {[from the parent game] The king's bishop, at his queen's bishop's fourth square.} Bc5
{[from the parent game] The same.} 3. c3 {The queen's bishop's pawn, one move.} Qh4 {The queen,
at the adverse king's rook's fourth square.} 4. Qe2 {The queen, at her king's second square.}
Nf6 {The king's knight, at his bishop's third square.} 5. d3 {The queen's pawn, one move.} Ng4
{The king's knight, at the adverse king's knight's fourth square.} 6. g3 {The king's knight's
pawn, one move, (a) NOTE: On this move, the king's knight might be played to his bishop's third
square.} Qf6 {The queen, at her king's bishop's third square, (b) NOTE: If, instead of
withdrawing his queen, he had taken your king's bishop's pawn, by giving check, you must have
taken his bishop with your queen; you would have got a piece by it.} 7. Nh3 {The king's knight,
at his rook's third square. (c) NOTE: Though the rook's third square is not the best place for
the getting out of the knights, it is nevertheless necessary in the present situation.} d6 {The
queen's pawn, one move.} 8. f3 {The king's bishop's pawn, one move.} Ne3 {The king's knight, at
the adverse king's third square. (d) NOTE: Had he made this knight retire, you must then have
played yours, now attacked, to his bishop's second square, and afterwards pushed your queen's
bishop to your king's third square, to get rid of his king's bishop.} 9. Bxe3 {The queen's
bishop takes the knight.} Bxe3 {The king's bishop takes the bishop.} 10. Qxe3 {The queen takes
the king's bishop.} Bxh3 {The queen's bishop takes the knight.} 11. Nd2 {The queen's knight, at
his queen's second square.} Nd7 {The same. (e) NOTE: It is plain that the white have several
moves over the black. The reason is, that the black have been too hasty in getting out the
queen, and that, in the present situation, she has not yet reached the place where the should
properly stand. O iv} *
